[00:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] Hi, welcome to another video. Before starting the video, I\'d like to tell you that many of the skills and tools that I talk about in this video has recently been covered on my second channel, AIC King. Like there\'s a new Archifi'

[00:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tutorial and many of the other stuff. So, please go ahead and check them out as well and subscribe to me there. So today I want to talk about how to get better results from GPT6 Astra, especially if you\'re using it to build'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'apps. These are the settings, skills, and prompts that I would recommend based on how I\'ve been using the model. Because Astra can do some really good work, but it can also write way more code than you need, spend time reading skills that aren\'t relevant, and give'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you a website that looks like every other AI generated website. You still need to give it some direction. So, I\'ll go through my setup, when I would increase the reasoning effort, and how I would get it to test and improve the finished app. Let\'s get right into it.'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The first thing is the reasoning setting. Personally, I mostly use Medium, and that includes planning. In the work I\'ve been doing, I haven\'t seen enough improvement from the higher reasoning settings to make them my default. Medium has been good at understanding the task, putting together'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a plan, and working through the implementation. Now, that\'s my experience with my tasks. You might have a project where higher reasoning makes a clear difference, but I would start with medium and see where it actually falls short. If the model keeps failing to fix'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a bug or there\'s a difficult part of the implementation that still isn\'t good enough, that\'s when I would turn the reasoning up. Give it the specific problem you want it to spend more effort on. For example, imagine a form that says it\'s saved successfully, but the'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data disappears when you refresh. Before changing the reasoning setting, give Astra the reproduction steps, the error messages if there are any, and what you\'ve already tried. You can say the form shows a success message, but'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'refreshing the page brings back the old value. Trace the save request and find where the new value gets lost. Reproduce the problem before changing the code. If medium still can\'t resolve that, then try a higher setting with the same'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'evidence. That gives you something useful to compare and think about the cost of completing the task. Extra reasoning can mean more waiting and more usage, but so can asking the model to try the same failed fix five times. I'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'would look at whether the result improves enough to justify that extra work. The same thing applies to planning. Tell it what you\'re building, who it\'s for, and what the first working version needs to do. If you\'re building'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a a personal expense tracker, say whether the data should stay on your device or sync across accounts. That\'s a decision the model needs before it can make a useful plan. Now, the next thing I recommend is the ponytail plugin.'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Astra can be quite verbose with code. Sometimes you ask for a small feature and the implementation comes back with extra layers, helper functions, and flexibility for things you haven\'t asked for. That\'s more code you have to understand and maintain later. Ponytail'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'encourages the agent to look for the simplest solution that actually handles the requirement. Use what\'s already available in the project. Prefer built-in functionality where it fits and avoid building for imaginary future'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'requirements. Let\'s say you\'re adding a settings page with a couple of toggles. I would ask Astra to check how the existing app handles settings and follow that pattern. I don\'t want a new state management library introduced just because we\'re adding two controls. A'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt here could be use ponytail for this implementation. Read the existing settings code first. Reuse the current components and make the smallest clear change that meets the requirement. Explain any new dependency you think is necessary. That last part is useful'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because sometimes an extra dependency is justified. You want the model to explain what problem it solves in this particular app. Also, fewer lines of code don\'t automatically mean a faster app. A short function can still make an expensive database query or repeatedly'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'download the same data. Simpler code helps you understand what\'s happening and will measure performance separately. Keep the validation and error handling the feature needs. If a settings change fails to save, the user should know'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'making the implementation smaller shouldn\'t remove behavior that makes the app work properly. So ponytail is a good option for controlling that tendency to overbuild. But that brings me to another tip, which is to be selective about'

[03:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'skills. Personally, I found Astra quite eager to read skills, and if you have a large collection available, it can spend time loading instructions that don\'t really help with what you\'re doing. In Codeex, the full text of every installed skill isn\'t automatically loaded. It'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'starts with the names and descriptions and loads the detailed instructions when it uses a skill. The thing to watch is how many skills it actually decides to read. Those instructions take up context, and overlapping instructions'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can send the model in different directions. You might have one skill telling it to keep working while another tells it to stop for a planning conversation. So, I would keep the skills relevant to the current task. Use your design guidance when working on the'

[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interface. Bring in Archify when you want to understand the architecture. Use browser testing tools when there\'s a running app to check. You can tell it. Use only the skills that directly help with this task. Read the relevant project instructions and avoid loading'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unrelated guides. And if it keeps stopping unexpectedly, ask it which instruction caused that. Sometimes the thing you need to fix is a rule in your setup. Adding another paragraph to your prompt can make an already confusing set of instructions even harder to follow.'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Anyway, now let\'s come to the visual side because this is where I would give Astra a lot more direction. If you just ask it to build a modern dashboard, you\'re leaving almost every design decision open, and you can end up with'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the familiar oversized cards, random gradients, and a layout that could belong to almost any product. Personally, I don\'t want that for an app I\'m putting in front of customers. I want the interface to look like somebody'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thought about the product and the people using it. So, give it visual references. You can use screenshots from dribble, websites you like, or something you\'ve already designed, but explain what you like about each reference. For example, you might say, "Use the compact table'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'layout from the first screenshot. I like the typography and spacing in the second screenshot. Keep the colors close to our existing brand." And make the main action easy to find. That gives the model actual decisions to follow. If you'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'give it several screenshots with completely different styles and say you like all of them, it still has to guess which parts belong together. Also, use references that fit your app. A beautiful landing page doesn\'t tell the model much about how a dashboard should'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'handle a 100 rows of data. Show it the kind of screen you\'re asking it to build. You can also use something like open design to work out the important screens first and then give Astra that visual direction for the implementation.'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you already have a design you\'re happy with, you can start from there. The Emil Kowalsski skills are another option that I use. They\'re useful for giving the agent more concrete guidance on design engineering and the details of'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how an interface behaves. I have a tutorial on those on my AIC King channel as well, so you can check that out for the setup. But even with a design skill, I would still give it a reference and explain the result I want.'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For a dashboard, I care about whether the information is easy to scan, whether the controls feel consistent, and whether it works when the screen gets smaller. Once the page is running, ask Astra to compare it with the reference.'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can say, "Check the spacing, typography, and layout against the screenshots. Fix the biggest differences and check the mobile view as well." That\'s much more actionable than repeatedly telling it to make the page'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look better. Now, another skill I recommend is Archify, especially when the project has reached a point where you don\'t fully understand how everything connects. You can use it to create an architecture diagram of your app, but ask it to inspect the actual'

[07:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'repository first. So the diagram reflects the implementation you have. For example, use Archifi to explain the architecture of this app based on the code. Show how the front end communicates with the back end, where'

[07:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the data is stored, and which external services are involved. Mark anything you couldn\'t verify. Then pick one thing a user does and follow it through the system. If they save an expense, which part of the interface handles that? Where does the request go? what writes'

[07:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it to the database and how does the updated information get back to the screen. Being able to follow that makes debugging much easier to discuss. You can point at the part of the flow that\'s failing and ask the agent to investigate'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. It\'s useful when you move the project to another agent as well. Give the next agent the diagram, a short explanation of the important decisions and the relevant code. That gives it a starting point for understanding the'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'project. And update the diagram when the architecture changes. If you\'ve replaced the storage layer, but the diagram still shows the old one. The next agent could start with the wrong assumptions, treat it as documentation that needs to stay'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connected to the code. Now, the last big tip is to use Astra\'s computer use capabilities to test your apps live. This is something I think the model is really good at. With browser or computer tools available in your setup, ask it to'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open the running app and go through the things a user would actually do. for that expense tracker. Have it complete the main flow using a test account. Include the filters and editing controls so it checks how the features work together. You can say, "Open the app and'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'complete the main user flow. Check that the data saves after a refresh. Try an empty form and an invalid value. Check the mobile layout. Fix the problems you find and repeat the affected steps. This is where you can catch things that a'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'successful build doesn\'t tell you. A button might be covered by another element on mobile. Or the form might accept a value that the back end rejects. Ask for a short report of what it tested, what failed, and what it couldn\'t check. If a service wasn\'t'

[09:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'available, that part should remain unverified. You want to know what the agent actually observed. And definitely ask it to look at loading times. A prompt like find out why this page loads slowly and improve it is worth trying, but I would make the measurement part'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'explicit. Tell it measure the initial load and the slowest interaction. Find the main bottleneck, make a targeted fix, and measure again under the same conditions. Report the before and after results depending on the tools available. That might involve checking'

[09:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'network requests, browser performance traces, or server timings. The useful part is finding where the time is going before making changes. For example, the app might request the same data several times. It might load large images where'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'small ones would work. Or the browser might be waiting on a slow backend response. Each of those needs a different fix. Keep the comparison fair as well. Comparing a fresh page load before the change with a cached page'

[10:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'load afterward can make an improvement look much bigger than it is. Use the same conditions and repeat the measurement enough to see whether the result is consistent. Then run through the affected flow again. If the dashboard loads faster but the filters'

[10:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'show stale data, the work isn\'t finished. Also, keep testing appropriate to the change. A small text edit needs a different level of checking from a change to how user data is saved. Ask it to complete the relevant checks and investigate further when those checks'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reveal a problem. There\'s one more instruction I would add if Astra keeps giving you a plan and then waiting for another message to start. Tell it how far you want it to go. For example, implement this feature and verify it locally. Make reasonable decisions about routine details and tell me what you'

[10:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'assumed. Ask when a missing answer would materially change the result. That gives it a clearer scope. If you want a working local app, say that. If you only want an architecture discussion, say that instead. The model has a much better chance of finishing at the right point when you\'ve defined what finished'

[10:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'means. So, let me put this together into an example. You could adapt for your own project. I would select medium reasoning, attach the relevant screenshots, and say something like this. Add an expense editing flow to this app. Inspect the existing'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'implementation and follow its patterns. Use ponytail to keep the change simple and load only the other skills relevant to the work. Follow the attached visual references for the form layout and spacing. Then, I would add the user should be able to edit an expense, save'

[11:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it, and see the updated value after refreshing. Show a useful message if the save fails. Implement the flow. Test it in the browser at desktop and mobile sizes and fix any failures you find. If the page also feels slow, I would make'

[11:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that a separate specific follow-up. Measure what\'s making this page slow, fix the main cause, and compare the results under the same conditions. And when I want to understand or hand over the project, that\'s when I would ask'

[11:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Archifi to map the current implementation. That\'s how I would use these tips together. Start with medium. Give the model a clear task and visual direction and use the skills that help with the work in front of it. Then check the app it actually built. If something still isn\'t good enough, you\'ve got a'

[12:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'specific problem to improve. Overall, it\'s pretty cool. Anyway, let me know your thoughts in the comments. If you like this video, consider donating through the super [music] thanks option or becoming a member by clicking the join button. Also, give this video a thumbs up and subscribe to my channel.'

[12:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll see you in the next one. Until then, bye. &gt;&gt; [music]'
